  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Nature and Physical Form
    
    Foedus∴vincu is a charter inscribed on pressed ice, kept in the deepest vault of Wane∴Oncea, the Nightfall Shelf's primary administrative complex, where volcanic heat keeps the air distorted and shimmering. The ice is described in every source as the color of old bone. It was created during the Great Thaw, the era of colonial expansion and guild formation, and authenticated by Dagger∴Vaesacsorius~, a seal-blade of considerable power. The relationship between charter and dagger is mutually constraining: the dagger cannot leave Nightfall Shelf without the charter's permission, and the charter cannot be amended without the dagger's witness. That a recovery warrant has since been issued for fragments of the dagger — with the ☽'ofund' Guild merchant Lovumruius~ Domina reportedly possessing the central shard — raises questions about the charter's current amendability that no source I have found addresses directly.
    
    ## Core Provisions
    
    The charter grants the ☽'ofund' Guild, the Nightfall Shelf's dominant fire-core trade organization, absolute monopoly over fire-core extraction and commerce within the Shelf. Penalties for violation include exile to the frozen sea or, more commonly, inscription on the Forgetting Stone and the burning of one's guild-mark. The charter subsumes Lex∴andatum, a subsidiary legal doctrine governing witness protections, and is central to the function of ☽'numu' Witness, an artifact whose role in authentication proceedings I have not been able to fully reconstruct.
    
    ## Wartime Commemorations
    
    After each major conflict, new lines are carved into the charter's ice recording the dead and the costs. The archive names six wars: the Vum∴tenebra, Bra∴conflictu, Mbra∴silentiu, Ofundis∴flux, Thron∴mbra Fracta, and Cav∴lapsum. The relationship index adds a seventh — the Pta∴extinct — which appears in no version of the descriptive text. The summary claims five. I note the discrepancy without resolving it; the ice-carvings themselves would settle the matter, but I have not been granted vault access.
    
    This accumulation of wartime amendments has transformed the charter from a trade monopoly instrument into what functions as Nightfall Shelf's de facto constitution.
    
    ## Enforcement and Scope Expansion
    
    The Shadow Parliament, Nightfall Shelf's governing body, claims enforcement authority. The Fire Keepers, who control the volcanic cores the charter regulates, exercise it in practice. The ☽'ofund' Guild has further extended the charter's coercive reach by invoking penalty provisions as leverage in tariff renegotiations — presenting fresh charter-carvings to compel compliance in routine commerce. The charter's authentication protocols also serve as credentials for wartime intelligence operations under Shadow Parliament authority.
    
    During the Vum∴tenebra, the ☽'shadow Covens' Order invoked charter provisions to classify its assault on E'frostling positions at Terrace Momiou as fire-core asset reclamation rather than conflict. No witness challenged the invocation under Lex∴andatum protections. The provisions stood technically uncontested while the charter's practical authority eroded — a distinction that matters only to lawyers, and only until the next war.
    
    ## The Xouhoulong Compact
    
    Light-Regent Xouhoulong of Aurora Stack drafted a compact using the charter's pressed-ice conventions, granting the Midnight Claws, a Nightshelf paramilitary faction, diplomatic recognition and passage through deep routes. This compact was authenticated not by Dagger∴Vaesacsorius~ but by the Directrix Mirror, an Aurora artifact. Xouhoulong cited Mandatum∴absconditu, the Nightshelf secrecy doctrine, as authority — a jurisdictional reach the Shadow Parliament rejected, arguing the doctrine governs only Nightshelf internal affairs.
    
    The deep-route tunnels opened under the compact's provisions were subsequently used by Wake-Singer orca to descend into the Glow-Fissure, a corruption source beneath the colony. A diplomatic instrument became an incursion corridor. The charter's conventions, designed to regulate fire-core trade, had been stretched to open the colony's foundations to its enemies.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771581422098_0", anchor: "This accumulation of wartime amendments has transformed the charter from a trade monopoly instrument", text: "The Nightshelf did not write a constitution and then suffer wars. They suffered wars and discovered they had written a constitution. The names of the dead are load-bearing — remove them and the legal framework collapses along with the ice. I have catalogued pressed sheets from the same era using identical carving technique. The cuts are structural. The document literally cannot stand without its wounds. I referenced this principle in my annotation on Zheishongfeihua's robes: damage reframed as design. Here it is at civilizational scale, and it is not funny anymore.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771581422098_1", anchor: "Xouhoulong cited Mandatum∴absconditu", text: "An Aurora light-regent citing a Nightshelf secrecy doctrine to authorize a compact the Shadow Parliament rejected. Her familiarity with Mandatum∴absconditu was not academic — see my annotation on the ☽'ofund' Guild sealed galleries. She knew the doctrine because she had operated under it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771581422098_2", anchor: "raises questions about the charter's current amendability that no source I have found addresses directly", text: "One source addresses it obliquely. A shadow-ledger entry from the second secret Flipper Accord renegotiation lists 'charter maintenance' as a deferred cost alongside fire-core shortfall projections. Amendability was already in question before the dagger fragmented. The fragments formalized a problem that predated them.", type: :correction, display: :popout
